  {
    "@id": "_:g14369940",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.timbrady.ca/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/sameAs": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.timbrady.ca/"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://www.facebook.com/bradyworks"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://www.instagram.com/_bradyworks_"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "http://www.timbrady.ca/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Bradyworks / Instruments of Happiness"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "“Bradyworks” was created in 1989 as the production team for composer and guitarts Tim Brady. In 2014 brady created the electric guitar collective “Instruments of Happiness” , which includes a quartet, and ensembles of 20 and 100 guitars, the latter used for large-scale spatialized performance. Their first CD - Instruments of Happiness - was released on the American label Starkland Records in March 2016, to excellent reviews. Their 100 and 20 guitar projects continue to play to sold-out houses across Canada, with concerts in Montreal and St. John's, a 100-guitar concert in Vancouver at the Modulus Festival in November 2016, and a new creation (homage to Jimi Hendrix) presented in Montreal in April 2017. Their first Canadian tour, with the IoH quartet, presented 8 concerts across the country in late March/early April 2017. 2018 saw a feature performance for 100 guitars at Toronto's Luminato, and a Canadian tour with singer Marie-Annick Béliveau. 2019 pulled out all the stops with their 2nd CD - The Happiness Handbook, a US tour and a massive 150-guitar event in Montréal to an audience of 3,200 people at the Festival MNM. Their CD \"The Happiness Handbook\" was awarded a Gold Medal by the Global Music Awards In the United States in August 2019, as well as a Prix Opus in Québec in January 2020.\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/PerformingGroup"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g14369960",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://chantslibres.org/en/welcome/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/sameAs": [
      {
        "@id": "https://chantslibres.org/en/welcome/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://chantslibres.org/en/welcome/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Chants Libres"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "CREATING TODAY WHAT WILL BE THE NORM TOMORROW \r\n\r\nFounded in 1990 by soprano Pauline Vaillancourt, in association with Joseph St-Gelais and Renald Tremblay, Chants Libres offers an operatic repertoire adapted to the colours of modernity, constantly exploring new techniques and approaches to vocal art, and working in close collaboration with creators and researchers of all disciplines. With nineteen creations to its credit, Chants Libres enjoys a solid reputation and the company has become an indispensable tool for creators and performers who wish to explore new forms and processes of..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/PerformingGroup"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g14370000",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/information-montreal-oct-1970"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2024"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "INFORMATION: Montréal Oct. 1970"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/information-montreal-oct-1970"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "ABOUT THE OPERA\r\nBased in part on real events and historical figures, the action of the opera takes place in Montreal in October 1970. The plot focuses on the relationship between two journalists: a young anglophone named Mary Vance, and the older, seasoned francophone reporter Sylvain Leclerc. The two reporters end up collaborating closely on coverage of the kidnapping of James Cross by the FLQ. Parallel to the events of the October Crisis, Mary is living her own personal crisis, as she is seeking to terminate an unwanted pregnancy, which was illegal in 1970.\r\n\r\n\r\n“There is a recurrent..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g14370240",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/NewsArticle"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/news/information-bradyworks-new-opera-tackles-october-crisis"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/author": [
