  {
    "@id": "_:g16523600",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/ironwood"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Ironwood"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/ironwood"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Before composing, I was lucky to have played freelance jazz guitar for nearly 10 years - nothing spectacular - just wallpaper gigs and small tours. When Tim Brady asked me to write a work for jazz guitarist Sam Wilson, I knew I wanted to write something very specific to jazz guitar. I asked Sam to send me some of her favourite pieces - which included varied works from Julian Lage to Roddy Ellias (who I was lucky to have as a teacher). To this I added some elements from my other jazz guitar heroes - Charlie Hunter, Ernest Ranglin, Ed Bickert and Grant Green (particularly Idle Moments). I..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523640",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/e"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "In E "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/e"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The title of this piece could have been \"Midlife Crisis Indulgence\" (“IN E”). I have always been fascinated by the guitar, but for some reason never had the guts to learn to play it. I started to do this a few years ago but my skills are still very basic. When Sam Wilson asked me to write a piece for her, I was bold enough to use my noodling as a starting point for my writing. Because it stems from my own performing limitations, the piece is in the guitar-for-dummies key of E minor. Hence the finally chosen title…&nbsp;"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523680",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/wave-ii"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Wave II"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/wave-ii"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "In June 2024, I sailed from the south west point of Svalbard, Norway to 80 degrees North, at the edge of the pack ice towards the North Pole. During my expedition I collected over 250 field recordings of ice, water and glaciers, and during the course of a year have been transcribing their various rhythmic patterns. Having spent an immense amount of time closely internalizing and embodying these water pulsations from the field recordings, I have discovered new perceptions in sound durations and time feel and how they relate to the instrument of guitar. By drawing from the healing and..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523720",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/sowing-spring-vignettes-flower-bone-spring-laments-blood-moon"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sowing Spring Vignettes (Flower Bone / Spring Laments / Blood Moon)"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/sowing-spring-vignettes-flower-bone-spring-laments-blood-moon"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Back in March I spent a week on the outskirts of St. George New Brunswick writing the initial seeds for this piece. On the day I arrived the river was completely frozen and the day I departed it was entirely melted. Over the course of my time there I heard it slowly cracking to creaking and breaking apart amidst the occasional crow call. The Sowing Spring Vignettes are inspired by this spring thaw and part of a larger collection that follows each season in a different Atlantic province.&nbsp;"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523760",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/oubaitori"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Oubaitori"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/oubaitori"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Le mot japonais oubaitori évoque quatre arbres - le cerisier, le prunier, le pêcher et le poirier - qui fleurissent chacun à leur propre rythme. Il rappelle que chaque être suit sa propre trajectoire. L'idée de choisir son propre épanouissement plutôt que la comparaison me parle beaucoup, et c'est ce que j'ai exploré dans cette pièce: les racines des quatre guitares vont lentement se déployer et se croiser à un rythme différent, tandis que la trompette gardera sa couleur tout en coexistant avec l'ensemble.&nbsp;"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523800",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/asgii-niimuu-aschii-niimuu-earth-dances"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "ᐊᔅᒌ ᓃᒨ aschii niimuu (The Earth Dances)"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/asgii-niimuu-aschii-niimuu-earth-dances"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "This piece is written for electric guitar quartet and a bass clarinet. The title of the work is reflective of my Cree culture and our traditional ability to hold spiritual communication with all things on this Earth. I often think of the Earth dancing as it orbits the sun. I have wondered what this celestial song must sound like. Is the song to which it sways too slow for us to hear? Does the Earth hum a tune too low for us to perceive? Does it dance to a cosmic voice singing a song in a literal and musical language that sounds to us like chaos? These questions, rhetorical as they may be,..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16523840",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/des-animaux-et-des-hommes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Des animaux et des hommes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/fr/le-vivier/repertoire/des-animaux-et-des-hommes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Des animaux et des hommes is inspired by visual artist Christian Michaud’s exhibition of the same name. His collection of small sculptures, often clay human heads placed within various contexts, invites critical reflection on the restrictive nature of social conventions and the destructive habits of human behavior, contrasted with the freedom of other living beings. The realism and surrealism of his work force the viewers to confront these themes directly, while marveling at the artist’s whimsical and thought-provoking imagination."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
